{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_pyzvkq7lfu4afcuz5475lsm63vwb47gbkt4hhby72337wns7wtca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The translation is complete and verified.\n\n## Surface enumeration\nThe C library is small: `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` globs `src/driver.c` into `libdriver.so`. `nm -D` on the C `.so` exports exactly two public symbols (no namespace/renaming macros in `include/driver.h`):\n\n- `driver` — declared in the public header\n- `fma_array` — non-static, so exported even though it isn't in the header\n\n`inner` is `static` (not exported); `memcpy`/`printf` are undefined imports.\n\n## What I wrote\n`translation/Cargo.toml` (`crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`) and `translation/src/lib.rs`:\n\n- `fma_array` / `driver` as `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\"` with exact signatures (`*mut c_int`, `*const c_int`, `c_int`).\n- `inner` kept private, matching the C `static`.\n- `out[i] = mul1[i]*mul2[i] + add[i]` uses `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` to reproduce what gcc actually emits for signed overflow (bug-for-bug, not \"fixed\").\n- The VLA `int out[len]` + `memcpy` became a heap `Vec` + `copy_nonoverlapping`; loop bounds use `c_int` comparison so non-positive `len` is a no-op exactly as in C.\n- **Printing goes through the platform `printf` via an `extern \"C\"` variadic declaration**, not `println!`. This matters: Rust's stdout is an independent `LineWriter`, so using it would reorder output relative to any other C stdio in the process and change buffering behavior when piped. Using libc `printf` with the same `\"%d\\n\"` literal keeps the byte stream and its interleaving identical.\n\n## Verification (my own, not self-reported)\n- `cargo build --release` — clean, no warnings.\n- Symbol gate: `comm -23` of the two sorted `nm -D --defined-only` lists is **empty** — Rust exports `driver` and `fma_array`, same as C.\n- Differential test: a `dlopen` harness ran both `.so`s over identical inputs — normal arrays, `len=0`, negative `len`, `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` overflow cases, a 1000-element array, plus harness-side `printf` calls interleaved to catch buffering divergence. `cmp` reports **byte-identical** across 1022 lines of output. (This interleaving check is what would have caught a `println!`-based implementation.)\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: `driver` with a negative `len` is UB in C (`memcpy` with a negative size becomes a huge `size_t` and segfaults, and the VLA is invalid). I chose to make it a safe no-op rather than replicate a crash, since the C behavior there isn't well-defined output. My harness confirms both libraries agree on `fma_array(..., -3)`; for `driver` with negative `len` the C version is not reliably reproducible, so it's the one input class where \"byte-identical\" is untestable. Test artifacts were removed; `c_src/` is untouched."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":312,"cache_read_input_tokens":22117,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":312,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e7c33da2-0696-4d87-b4d2-d259bd8abd9f","uuid":"4ef1b5a5-117e-4d19-bed7-85d1002090dd","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:07:03.127Z"}
